<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhopawar Agency</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Bhopawar Agency of Central India</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhopawar Agency was a political agency of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-india-agency">Central India Agency</a> covering the hilly, largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> country of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. A British political agent supervised its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, including Jhabua, Alirajpur, Barwani and many minor chiefships such as Kathiwara and Mathwar. After independence its states joined the union of Madhya Bharat in 1948.</p></div>
